  "worked_solution": "First, we select the identity that contains a ${tan}^{2}\u2061x$ term. So we can use the identity \n\n$$\n1+{tan}^{2}\u2061x={sec}^{2}\u2061x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we compute the given integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{sec}\u2061x}{1+{tan}^{2}\u2061x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{sec}\u2061x}{{sec}^{2}\u2061x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sec}\u2061x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\operatorname{cos}\u2061x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$",
